   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.2. Increased capacity of the member States to apply international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Amendments to the Industrial Relation Act largely entered into force on 1 January 2021, while amendments to the Employees&#x2019; Minimum Standards of Housing &amp; Amenities Act entered into force on 1 June 2020. Amendments to other key labour laws, including the Trade Unions Act, the Employment Act and the Occupational Safety and Health Act, are pending tabling in Parliament. 

On the application to C019 - Equality of Treatment (Accident Compensation) Convention, 1925 (No. 19), the CEACR notes with satisfaction the information provided by the Government that foreign workers are now covered under the Employees&#x2019; Social Security Act. and Social Security Office coverage is extended to cover injury compensation for migrant workers.
https://www.ilo.org/wcmsp5/groups/public/---ed_norm/---relconf/documents/meetingdocument/wcms_771042.pdf )
 The ILO provided formal and comprehensive written comments on draft amendments to the various labour laws. 

It held a series of meetings and expert consultations with the Ministry on labour law reform.  It also provided technical notes and written advice to the Ministry of Human Resources on selected key issues related to the legal reform. 
Besides, the ILO organized tripartite meetings and dialogue sessions as well as capacity building programmes on labour law reform for each of the constituents. 
The ILO coordinated and conducted the Direct Contacts Mission on the application of the C 19 in October 2019, in collaboration with the Ministry of Human Resources.</narrative>
   </description>
